there is a an old poem that starts it is  an ill wind that blows no good and I  like to think that Katrina was not until  win  over the 10 15 years prior to Katrina we  had a pretty much the worst public  school system for a city of its size in  the United States if you look at public  education and how its organized you  basically have small monopolies that  enjoy a geographic spread those  monopolies have entered into many unholy  alliances with labor and as a result  it's extremely difficult to change the  equation to change the dynamic and to  change the atmosphere for creativity and  innovation to thrive  well 2003 the state created something  called a recovery school district and it  was designed to address the fact that  some schools were object ly low  achieving schools for very long periods  of time normal state takeovers would  embody a new superintendent coming in  and trying to run the schools just as  the old superintendent did but in this  case we decided to charter the schools  the beauty of the recovery school  district is that by taking over the  schools we took them over into a new  entity the state the recovery school  district in the it did not have a  collective bargaining agreement so it  was a very small effort and it was  pretty much ignored by  the union's at the time that would of  course change later when the larger  takeover took place  so on August 29th you had 126 schools  and the next day you had none and by the  end of that first school year you only  had six schools operating in the city of  New Orleans after the storm I think a  lot of people looked at the whole city  of New Orleans as an opportunity to  reset the table how could we find a  silver lining in this very dark cloud  that was Katrina the governor then  Kathleen Blanco very courageously went  against her base created this takeover  there was an attempt in the legislature  to attach collective bargaining to it if  that was rejected and then we moved  forward and we move forward chartering  schools we got a lot of help from the  outside so that was the strategy now  interestingly after about a year and a  half there was a lot of counter pressure  from the community and it really started  getting messy the experimentation was  scary to people and people were very  very resistant and they started getting  their political strength back again and  started pushing back to the status quo  and that's when I came in and my role in  all of that really was to create an  environment where these third parties  could thrive  now most charters in the country operate  in very toxic environments even if you  have a good charter when you're battling  against the school district and the  school district is trying to outmaneuver  you and they are much bigger and much  more agile in being able to do that it's  hard for charters to survive but here in  New Orleans charters are the dominant  player on the landscape and we tried to  create a protective environment not  because their charters but because there  are schools and we've tried to give them  an even opportunity to operate and I  think when you do that I think charters  will prevail for the obvious reason that  they create an environment where  innovation and creativity thrive far  more than half in fact almost  three-quarters of the schools in the  city of New Orleans come next year will  be charter schools and over the long  haul  we hope all will be charged schools a  traditional school system has a school  board a superintendent superintendent  has a big staff the staff oversees  principals tells them what to do when to  do it how to do it what we've tried to  do in New Orleans is create a management  structure that oversees schools in a  fundamentally different way instead of  managing how they run their school  building we try to create a climate  where they can run their school building  and where they have control the  principals have control over the  finances they have control over the  human capital and they have control over  the academic agenda and then what we say  to them is you need to meet a level of  performance we've seen anywhere from  two-thirds to three-quarters of our  school Excel at rates of growth that far  outstrip other schools on average in the  state of Louisiana  but you do see some schools which don't  do well so now we have something called  a transformational school process where  each year we target four of the lowest  achieving schools regardless of whether  they're a charter or a traditional  school and we say we're gonna take you  out of business  prior to the storm very few people in  the city of New Orleans who sent their  children to public schools had any  interest in charter schools after the  storm and early on when charter schools  were just developing there was a  tremendous amount of resistance because  of philosophical beliefs but now that  people have seen success in charter  schools while they may not understand  them and while they may be told that  they're bad they want to send their kids  to charter schools I wouldn't say that  charter schools are the answer  I would say they can be an answer and  more likely to be an answer in the right  environment but traditional schools can  be as well if the district office gives  the kind of autonomy to a traditional  school the Legislature passed a bill to  allow for what amounts to a voucher we  call it a scholarship where children who  are going to the lowest achieving  schools in our city in the recovery  school district are able to take dollars  and go purchase and education at a  private or parochial school as we gain  more success the opposition grows more  vigorous you know they're they're  starting a major ad campaign here in  Louisiana to try and  some of these reforms the School Board  Association is doing the same thing so  those two enterprises are probably oddly  enough in partnership working with each  other management and labor are teaming  up to try and beat this back so the  outcome is still in doubt but I am  reasonably optimistic
 

